## css: Garlic's CSS parser, selector matching and cascade
## (docs/design/garlic-browser.md, "CSS on cells").
##
## Written from CSS Syntax Level 3 (tokenizing, simplified), Selectors
## Level 4 (type, universal, `#id`, `.class`, `[attr]` with operators,
## structural pseudo-classes, `:not()`, `:is()`, `:where()`, the four
## combinators, `,` lists), CSS Nesting 1 (`&`, nested rules and at-rules),
## CSS Cascade Levels 4 and 5 (specificity, order, `!important`, inline
## `style`, `@layer` order) and Conditional Rules 3 (`@supports`). Other
## at-rules are skipped with their blocks; `@media` rules apply when the
## query matches the window (Media Queries Level 4, simplified:
## `media_matches_env`).
##
## Dynamic pseudo-classes (`:hover`, `:focus`, `:active`, `:visited`,
## `:target` and kin) parse but never hold: Garlic has no pointer or focus
## state in the cascade, so `:not(:focus)` holds on every element and a
## selector that needs `:hover` is dropped at parse (it could never match).

use html

## One declaration: `name: value [!important]`, the name lowercase. The
## value is lowercase except inside quoted strings and `url(...)`.
pub struct Decl
    pub name str
    pub value str
    pub important bool

## The simple selectors that must all hold on one element: an optional
## type, ids, classes, attribute tests and structural pseudo-classes.
pub struct Neg
    pub tag str
    pub ids List[str]
    pub classes List[str]
    pub attrs List[AttrTest]
    pub pseudos List[str]

## `:is()`/`:where()` (`negate` false: one of `sels` must match the
## element) or `:not()` with a list or complex selectors (`negate` true:
## none may). `weight` is the specificity it adds: the largest of `sels`,
## 0 for `:where()` (Selectors 4, 17).
pub struct Alts
    pub negate bool
    pub sels List[Selector]
    pub weight int

## A compound selector: a `Neg`'s tests plus the simple `:not(...)` ones,
## none of which may hold, and the `:is()`/`:where()`/`:not(list)` ones.
## A pseudo-class that never holds is stored as the pseudo "never".
pub struct Compound
    pub tag str
    pub ids List[str]
    pub classes List[str]
    pub attrs List[AttrTest]
    pub pseudos List[str]
    pub nots List[Neg]
    pub alts List[Alts]

## `[name]` (`op` empty) or `[name op value]` with `op` one of `=`, `~=`,
## `|=`, `^=`, `$=`, `*=`.
pub struct AttrTest
    pub name str
    pub op str
    pub value str

## A complex selector: compounds left to right; `comb[k]` is the
## combinator between compound k and k+1: " ", ">", "+" or "~".
pub struct Selector
    pub parts List[Compound]
    pub comb List[str]
    pub specificity int

## A style rule; `order` is its position, for the cascade. `media` holds
## the media queries it sits in, one per line, all of which must match;
## "" for none. `layer` indexes its sheet's `layers` (-1: unlayered).
pub struct Rule
    pub selector Selector
    pub decls List[Decl]
    pub order int
    pub media str
    pub layer int

## A parsed style sheet: rules indexed by their rightmost compound's id,
## first class or type, so matching an element tries only likely rules.
## `layers` names the cascade layers in their order (Cascade 5, 6.4):
## a rule in a later layer wins over one in an earlier layer.
pub struct Sheet
    pub rules List[Rule]
    pub by_id Map[str, List[int]]
    pub by_class Map[str, List[int]]
    pub by_tag Map[str, List[int]]
    pub general List[int]
    pub layers List[str]

## Where the parser is: the media queries, layer and enclosing style
## rule's selector list ("" at the top level) of the rules it reads.
struct Nest
    media str
    layer int
    layer_name str
    parent str
    start int

## The window media queries are asked about: CSS pixels and colour scheme.
struct Media
    w int
    h int
    dark bool

## The properties Garlic lays out or paints; the rest are dropped at parse
## (custom properties `--x` are always kept).
